 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "a1",
   "source_id": "we can either get one or two minima",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Analyze",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "regular-solutions",
   "prompt": "<p>For a regular solution, $\\kDeltamixG/\\kR\\kT = x\\ln x + (1-x)\\ln(1-x) + \\kareg x(1-x)$ with $x = \\kxone$.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Find the second derivative of $\\kDeltamixG/\\kR\\kT$ at $x = 0.5$. Above what value of $\\kareg$ is it negative, so that the curve has two minima?</li><li>With $\\kWint = 5.0$ kJ/mol and $\\kareg = \\kWint/\\kR\\kT$, at what temperature is the consolute point?</li></ol>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 2,
      "hint": "Differentiate twice: x ln x gives 1/x."
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": 300.7,
      "unit": "K",
      "hint": "Set W/RT equal to the value from part a."
     }
    ],
    "solution": "<p>(a) The second derivative is $1/x + 1/(1-x) - 2\\kareg$, which at $x = 0.5$ is $4 - 2\\kareg$. It is negative for $\\kareg > 2$: the middle of the curve then bulges upward and the single minimum splits into two.</p><p>(b) The consolute point is at $\\kareg = 2$, so $\\kT = \\kWint/2\\kR = (5000\\ \\text{J/mol})/(2 \\times 8.314\\ \\text{J K}^{-1}\\text{mol}^{-1}) = 300.7$ K. Below it the two liquids separate; above it they mix in every proportion.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
